<!-- Airhub vs Orange Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set airhubPrimary = providerA.codes | findCode("ESIMOFFER5") %}
  {% set orangePrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you just need data and want the cheapest per-GB price with a working discount code. Pick <strong>Orange</strong> if you want real phone calls and texting bundled in as standard, not sold separately, and you're willing to pay a lot more for it. They're not really competing for the same traveler.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Cheap Data-Only vs a Carrier's Full Bundle</h2>
  <p>
    Airhub sells fixed data blocks with SMS and a local number thrown in, no voice calls, at some of the lowest per-GB prices on this site. Orange Travel is the international eSIM arm of Orange, one of Europe's largest telecom groups, and its plans include unlimited calls, unlimited SMS, and hotspot tethering standard, not as a paid add-on. That bundle is also why Orange sits in our "Expensive" price bracket while Airhub sits in "Cheap": you're paying for carrier-grade voice service, not just data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Airhub's own code, <strong>ADAMANDLINDS</strong>, currently isn't applying a discount at checkout (we've reported it to Airhub). The public code <strong>ESIMOFFER5</strong> is the one we've confirmed actually knocks 5% off before payment, so that's the code to use. Orange has no promo code system at all in its checkout; the code we previously listed for it, AW0, doesn't appear anywhere on the real site.
  </p>

  <h2>Price Snapshot (before any code)</h2>
  <div class="content-box">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Destination</th>
          <th>Airhub</th>
          <th>Orange</th>
