        <tr><td>France</td><td>$3.50 (3GB)</td><td>$5.99 (1GB)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Italy</td><td>$5 (3GB)</td><td>$8.99 (1GB)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Europe regional</td><td>$4.50 (4GB)</td><td>$5.99 (1GB)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Global</td><td>$11.50 (3GB)</td><td>$52.99 (20GB)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="content-note">Even before Airhub's code, it's cheaper per gigabyte almost everywhere we checked. Orange's Europe plans do get automatic strike-through markdowns on bigger tiers (up to roughly 40% off list price on its largest 90-day/500GB plan), but that's a volume discount for a long stay, not a way to match Airhub's rate on a short trip.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Discount Codes</h2>
  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Airhub:</strong> ESIMOFFER5, {{ airhubPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/airhub/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Orange:</strong> No working code. <a href="/orange/">See why &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Airhub's own branded code (ADAMANDLINDS) isn't applying a discount right now, so use the public ESIMOFFER5 code instead. Orange doesn't run a promo-code checkout field; its only real "discount" is the automatic sale pricing built into bigger plans.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Airhub or Orange the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airhub if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You only need data, not a working phone number for calls</li>
        <li>You want the lowest realistic per-GB price with a code that actually works</li>
        <li>Your trip is short: Airhub's minimum plan validity is 5 days against Orange's 14</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Orange if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You need real unlimited calls and texts, not an add-on or a separate purchase</li>
        <li>You're staying in Europe specifically and want a carrier-backed network behind the eSIM</li>
        <li>You're booking a long, high-data stay where the automatic bulk markdown actually kicks in</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Airhub's plans last up to a year after purchase; Orange's expire in 6 months.</strong> If you buy ahead of a trip that might get delayed or extended, Airhub gives you a lot more room before the data goes stale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>"190+ destinations" and "200+ destinations" aren't the same kind of claim here.</strong> Airhub's figure covers data-only eSIMs across that whole range. Orange's broader marketing number covers its full network, but the calls/SMS/hotspot bundle and the pricing above is specific to its Europe plan lineup, 39 named countries and territories, not a flat global rate. If calls and texts matter to you outside Europe, check Orange's plan page for that destination directly before assuming the same bundle applies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither provider is the cheapest option on this site if raw per-GB price is all you care about: see <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a> for that. And if you'd rather pay one flat unlimited price than track either provider's GB buckets, see <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Airhub vs Orange FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airhub or Orange</h3>
      <p>Airhub, by a wide margin on a per-gigabyte basis, with a working 5% code on top. Orange only closes part of that gap on its biggest, longest plans, where an automatic bulk discount kicks in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Airhub include phone calls</h3>
      <p>No. Airhub bundles SMS and a local number, but voice calls aren't included. Orange's Europe plans include unlimited calls and SMS standard.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Is there a working Orange discount code</h3>
      <p>Not one we could verify. Orange's checkout has no promo code field at all; its discounts are automatic strike-through sale prices on bigger plans instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one should I use outside Europe</h3>
      <p>Airhub, generally. Orange's calls/SMS/hotspot bundle and pricing we've verified is specific to its Europe plan lineup; check its own site for your exact destination before assuming the same bundle applies elsewhere.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

